." Manpage for xdlfcn.h
.TH XDLFCN 3 "2026" "Extern Library" "Programmer's Manual"

.SH NAME
xdlfcn - cross-platform dynamic library loading and symbol lookup

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include "xdlfcn.h"

.BR "char *xdllibpath(const char *dir, const char *name);"
.br
.BR "int xdladdr(const void *addr, XDl_info *info);"
.br
.BR "HMODULE xdlopen(const char *file, int mode);"
.br
.BR "HMODULE xwdlopen(const wchar_t *file, int mode);"
.br
.BR "FARPROC xdlsym(HMODULE handle, const char *name);"
.br
.BR "FARPROC xwdlsym(HMODULE handle, const wchar_t *name);"
.br
.BR "char *xdlerror(void);"
.br
.BR "wchar_t *xwdlerror(void);"
.br
.BR "int xdlclose(HMODULE handle);"
.br
.BR "FARPROC GetProcAddressA(HMODULE hModule, LPCSTR lpProcName);"
.br
.BR "FARPROC GetProcAddressW(HMODULE hModule, LPCWSTR lpProcName);"

.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B xdlfcn.h
library provides a cross-platform interface for dynamically loading
shared libraries, looking up exported symbols, reporting loader errors,
and unloading libraries.

On POSIX systems, the interface wraps the standard
.BR dlopen (3),
.BR dlsym (3),
.BR dlerror (3),
and
.BR dlclose (3)
interfaces.

On Windows, the interface uses the Windows dynamic-loader APIs
.BR LoadLibraryA (),
.BR LoadLibraryW (),
.BR GetProcAddress (),
.BR GetModuleHandleA (),
.BR GetModuleHandleW (),
and
.BR FreeLibrary ().

.SS LOADER FLAGS

The header defines portable
.B XRTLD_*
constants corresponding to dynamic-loader flags.

.TP
.B XRTLD_LAZY
Resolve symbols lazily where supported.

On Windows, there is no distinct equivalent because symbol resolution
follows normal Windows PE loader behavior.

.TP
.B XRTLD_NOW
Resolve required symbols when the library is loaded.

There is no distinct Windows equivalent.

.TP
.B XRTLD_BINDING_MASK
Mask for dynamic-loader binding flags.

.TP
.B XRTLD_NOLOAD
Do not load a new library. Obtain a handle to an already loaded library.

On Windows this is implemented using
.BR GetModuleHandleA ()
or
.BR GetModuleHandleW ().

.TP
.B XRTLD_DEEPBIND
Request deep symbol binding where supported.

There is no direct Windows equivalent.

.TP
.B XRTLD_NODELETE
Request that a loaded library remain mapped after unloading where
supported.

.TP
.B XRTLD_LOCAL
Do not make symbols from the loaded library globally available.

.TP
.B XRTLD_GLOBAL
Make symbols from the loaded library globally available where supported.

.TP
.B XRTLD_FIRST
Request first-level symbol lookup where supported.

.TP
.B XRTLD_NEXT
Special symbol lookup handle corresponding to
.BR RTLD_NEXT
where supported.

.TP
.B XRTLD_DEFAULT
Special symbol lookup handle corresponding to
.BR RTLD_DEFAULT
where supported.

.SS xdlopen()

.BR "HMODULE xdlopen(const char *file, int mode);"

Loads a dynamic library using a narrow-character filename.

On Windows, the function uses
.BR LoadLibraryA ().

When
.B XRTLD_NOLOAD
is specified, the function uses
.BR GetModuleHandleA ()
instead of loading a new module.

On POSIX systems, the function calls
.BR dlopen (3).

The
.I mode
argument is a combination of
.B XRTLD_*
flags.

Returns a module handle on success and
.BR NULL
on failure.

.SS xwdlopen()

.BR "HMODULE xwdlopen(const wchar_t *file, int mode);"

Wide-character equivalent of
.BR xdlopen ().

On Windows, the function uses
.BR LoadLibraryW ().

When
.B XRTLD_NOLOAD
is specified, it uses
.BR GetModuleHandleW ().

On POSIX systems, the filename is converted using
.BR xwcstombs ()
and passed to
.BR dlopen (3).

Returns a module handle on success and
.BR NULL
on failure.

.SS xdlsym()

.BR "FARPROC xdlsym(HMODULE handle, const char *name);"

Looks up an exported symbol in a loaded dynamic library.

On Windows, the function uses
.BR GetProcAddress ().

On POSIX systems, it uses
.BR dlsym (3).

The
.I handle
argument must identify a loaded module, and
.I name
must contain the symbol name.

Returns the address of the symbol on success and
.BR NULL
on failure.

.SS xwdlsym()

.BR "FARPROC xwdlsym(HMODULE handle, const wchar_t *name);"

Wide-character equivalent of
.BR xdlsym ().

The symbol name is converted to the platform's multibyte representation
before performing the lookup.

On Windows, the converted name is passed to
.BR GetProcAddress ().

On POSIX systems, it is passed to
.BR dlsym (3).

Returns the address of the symbol on success and
.BR NULL
on failure.

.SS xdlerror()

.BR "char *xdlerror(void);"

Returns a description of the most recent dynamic-loader error.

On Windows, the function obtains the error code using
.BR GetLastError ()
and converts it into a human-readable string using
.BR FormatMessageA ().

The Windows error buffer is thread-local.

On POSIX systems, the function directly returns the result of
.BR dlerror (3).

Returns
.BR NULL
when no error is available.

On Windows, calling
.BR xdlerror ()
consumes the current error by resetting it to
.BR ERROR_SUCCESS .

.SS xwdlerror()

.BR "wchar_t *xwdlerror(void);"

Wide-character equivalent of
.BR xdlerror ().

On Windows, the error message is generated using
.BR FormatMessageW ()
and stored in a thread-local buffer.

On POSIX systems, the result of
.BR dlerror (3)
is converted to a wide-character string using
.BR xmbstowcs ().

Returns a pointer to an error message or
.BR NULL
when no error is available.

.SS xdlclose()

.BR "int xdlclose(HMODULE handle);"

Unloads a previously loaded dynamic library.

On Windows, the function uses
.BR FreeLibrary ().

On POSIX systems, it uses
.BR dlclose (3).

Returns zero on success and
.BR -1
on failure.

.SH WINDOWS-COMPATIBLE WRAPPERS

.SS GetProcAddressA()

.BR "FARPROC GetProcAddressA(HMODULE hModule, LPCSTR lpProcName);"

Looks up an exported symbol using a narrow-character symbol name.

On Windows, this function calls the native
.BR GetProcAddress ()
API.

On POSIX systems, it is implemented using
.BR xdlsym ().

.SS GetProcAddressW()

.BR "FARPROC GetProcAddressW(HMODULE hModule, LPCWSTR lpProcName);"

Wide-character version of
.BR GetProcAddressA ().

On Windows, the wide-character symbol name is converted before calling
the native
.BR GetProcAddress ()
API.

On POSIX systems, it is implemented using
.BR xwdlsym ().

.SS POSIX COMPATIBILITY WRAPPERS

On non-Windows systems, the header provides the following Windows-style
interfaces:

.BR "HMODULE GetModuleHandleA(const char *lpModuleName);"
.br
.BR "HMODULE GetModuleHandleW(const wchar_t *lpModuleName);"
.br
.BR "HMODULE LoadLibraryA(const char *lpLibFileName);"
.br
.BR "HMODULE LoadLibraryW(const wchar_t *lpLibFileName);"
.br
.BR "FARPROC GetProcAddress(HMODULE hModule, LPCSTR lpProcName);"

.BR GetModuleHandleA ()
is implemented using
.BR xdlopen ()
with
.BR XRTLD_NOLOAD.

.BR GetModuleHandleW ()
is implemented using
.BR xwdlopen ()
with
.BR XRTLD_NOLOAD.

.BR LoadLibraryA ()
is implemented using
.BR xdlopen ()
with
.BR XRTLD_LAZY.

.BR LoadLibraryW ()
is implemented using
.BR xwdlopen ()
with
.BR XRTLD_LAZY.

.BR GetProcAddress ()
is implemented using
.BR GetProcAddressA ().

.SH EXAMPLE

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include "xdlfcn.h"

int main(void)
{
HMODULE handle;
FARPROC symbol;

```
handle = xdlopen("libexample.so", XRTLD_NOW);

if (!handle) {
    char *error = xdlerror();

    if (error)
        fprintf(stderr, "xdlopen: %s\\n", error);

    return 1;
}

symbol = xdlsym(handle, "example_function");

if (!symbol) {
    char *error = xdlerror();

    if (error)
        fprintf(stderr, "xdlsym: %s\\n", error);

    xdlclose(handle);
    return 1;
}

/*
 * Convert/use symbol according to its known function type.
 */

xdlclose(handle);
return 0;
```

}
.fi

.SH ERROR HANDLING

On Windows, dynamic-loader errors are obtained from
.BR GetLastError ().

.BR xdlerror ()
and
.BR xwdlerror ()
convert Windows error codes into human-readable messages.

On POSIX systems, dynamic-loader errors are reported through
.BR dlerror (3).

.SH THREAD SAFETY

On Windows,
.BR xdlerror ()
and
.BR xwdlerror ()
use thread-local buffers.

On POSIX systems, error handling follows the guarantees of the
underlying dynamic-loader implementation.

A module must not be unloaded while another thread may still execute
code or access symbols originating from that module.

.SH CROSS-PLATFORM NOTES

.TS
tab(@);
l l.
Feature@Implementation
Narrow library loading@LoadLibraryA() / dlopen()
Wide library loading@LoadLibraryW() / xwcstombs() + dlopen()
Symbol lookup@GetProcAddress() / dlsym()
Library unloading@FreeLibrary() / dlclose()
Error reporting@GetLastError() / dlerror()
No-load lookup@GetModuleHandle*() / RTLD_NOLOAD
.TE

The Windows loader does not provide direct equivalents for all POSIX
dynamic-loader flags. In particular,
.BR XRTLD_LAZY ,
.BR XRTLD_NOW ,
and
.BR XRTLD_DEEPBIND
do not have distinct Windows loader semantics.

